export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"partcraft","addon":"fem-solid","name":"Solid FEM","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"fem-solid-start","scope":"addon:fem-solid","title":"Solid FEM: stress and displacement of a part","summary":"Switch the Solid FEM add-on on, mesh the part, pick fixed faces and loads in the 3D view, solve, and read displacement, von Mises stress, reactions and natural frequencies drawn on the model.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["solid fem","finite element","fea","stress","von mises","displacement","reactions","natural frequency","modal","mesh","tetrahedron","add-on","simulation"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-study","text":"Your first study","level":2},{"id":"natural-frequencies","text":"Natural frequencies","level":2},{"id":"big-models","text":"Big models","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESolid FEM is a linear finite element study of a PartCraft part, with its own panel. It is an add-on: it comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you switch it on in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolid FEM\u003C/span\u003E. Its tab, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolid FEM\u003C/span\u003E, is then in the right panel. PartCraft&#039;s own Simulation workspace is not changed or replaced; Solid FEM is a second, separate way to look at the same part, with the CadCraft finite element library (quadratic tetrahedra).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is an analysis aid for an engineer, not a design approval. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-study\"\u003EYour first study\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EBuild a solid part, for example a bar. Open the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolid FEM\u003C/span\u003E tab.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChoose the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMaterial\u003C/span\u003E. Steel, stainless, aluminium, titanium and ABS are typical room-temperature values; pick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECustom\u003C/span\u003E and type your own E, Poisson ratio, density and yield stress.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd fixed faces\u003C/span\u003E, then click the faces of the part in the 3D view that are held (the face under the pointer lights up; click again to take one off). Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDone picking\u003C/span\u003E. Fixed holds all three directions of every point on those faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAdd a load: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd force\u003C/span\u003E (a total force in newtons spread evenly over the picked faces, with X, Y and Z components), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd pressure\u003C/span\u003E (MPa, positive pushes into the part) or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd gravity\u003C/span\u003E (the part&#039;s own weight; it needs a density).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMesh the part\u003C/span\u003E. The element size is automatic unless you type one; the line under the buttons says how many elements and unknowns there are. Press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolve\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERead the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EResults\u003C/span\u003E: the largest displacement and von Mises stress, the support reactions with the applied load beside them (their difference is shown, and a warning appears if it is not tiny), and the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWarnings\u003C/span\u003E list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPlot on the model\u003C/span\u003E choose von Mises stress or displacement, a deformation scale (automatic draws the largest displacement as 5 % of the part&#039;s size) and whether to place the plot on the part or beside it, then press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShow on the model\u003C/span\u003E. Blue is the lowest value, red the highest; the legend gives the range. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERemove the plot\u003C/span\u003E takes it away.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery change is one undo step, the settings are saved with the part, and the warnings also go to the Problems panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"natural-frequencies\"\u003ENatural frequencies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EChoose \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ENatural frequencies\u003C/span\u003E under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EStudy\u003C/span\u003E, set the number of modes, and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESolve\u003C/span\u003E. The frequencies are listed in hertz and each mode shape can be drawn on the model. The material needs a density, and the part needs fixed faces.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"big-models\"\u003EBig models\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe page solves up to 8,000 elements. A bigger model should be run on a workstation; the community check SU.908c-HAND on the roadmap collects those runs.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is Solid FEM is a linear finite element study of a PartCraft part, with its own panel. It is an add-on: it comes with PartCraft, is free, and is off until you switch it on in Settings › General › Add-ons › Solid FEM. Its tab, Solid FEM, is then in the right panel. PartCraft's own Simulation workspace is not changed or replaced; Solid FEM is a second, separate way to look at the same part, with the CadCraft finite element library (quadratic tetrahedra). It is an analysis aid for an engineer, not a design approval. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results. Your first study Build a solid part, for example a bar. Open the Solid FEM tab. Choose the Material. Steel, stainless, aluminium, titanium and ABS are typical room-temperature values; pick Custom and type your own E, Poisson ratio, density and yield stress. Press Add fixed faces, then click the faces of the part in the 3D view that are held (the face under the pointer lights up; click again to take one off). Press Done picking. Fixed holds all three directions of every point on those faces. Add a load: Add force (a total force in newtons spread evenly over the picked faces, with X, Y and Z components), Add pressure (MPa, positive pushes into the part) or Add gravity (the part's own weight; it needs a density). Press Mesh the part. The element size is automatic unless you type one; the line under the buttons says how many elements and unknowns there are. Press Solve. Read the Results: the largest displacement and von Mises stress, the support reactions with the applied load beside them (their difference is shown, and a warning appears if it is not tiny), and the Warnings list. Under Plot on the model choose von Mises stress or displacement, a deformation scale (automatic draws the largest displacement as 5 % of the part's size) and whether to place the plot on the part or beside it, then press Show on the model. Blue is the lowest value, red the highest; the legend gives the range. Remove the plot takes it away. Every change is one undo step, the settings are saved with the part, and the warnings also go to the Problems panel. Natural frequencies Choose Natural frequencies under Study, set the number of modes, and press Solve. The frequencies are listed in hertz and each mode shape can be drawn on the model. The material needs a density, and the part needs fixed faces. Big models The page solves up to 8,000 elements. A bigger model should be run on a workstation; the community check SU.908c-HAND on the roadmap collects those runs."},{"slug":"fem-solid-limits","scope":"addon:fem-solid","title":"Solid FEM: what the numbers mean and where they stop","summary":"How the stresses are smoothed, what the warnings say, what has been checked against closed-form answers and against PartCraft's own solver, and what the add-on does not do.","section":"trouble","order":2,"keywords":["solid fem","limits","accuracy","singular","stress concentration","mesh refinement","yield","linear","warnings","checked"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-solves","text":"What it solves","level":2},{"id":"reading-the-stress","text":"Reading the stress","level":2},{"id":"the-warnings","text":"The warnings","level":2},{"id":"what-it-has-been-checked-against","text":"What it has been checked against","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-solves\"\u003EWhat it solves\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ELinear elastic, isotropic material, small displacements, static or natural frequencies. It does not do contact, yielding, large deformation, buckling, heat or flow. For yielding and contact use the Advanced simulation add-on.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"reading-the-stress\"\u003EReading the stress\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe stress is computed in each element and the stress tensors are averaged at the nodes they share; the plot and the first number use that average. The second number, unsmoothed, is the largest value found at an element node before averaging. When they differ a lot, the peak is sharp or the mesh is coarse there.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EStress at a fixed face, at a point load and at a sharp inside corner grows as the mesh is refined, because the model has a singularity there. Refine the mesh (a smaller element size) and compare; do not read a single peak as the stress in the part. A pressure or force spread over a face is smoother than a fixed edge.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-warnings\"\u003EThe warnings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ENothing holds the part, or it can still move or turn: it will not solve; add fixed faces.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA coarse mesh (under 200 elements), stress above the yield stress, a displacement over 5 % of the part&#039;s size, or reactions that do not balance the loads: the result is shown with a warning that says what to do.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe list also goes to the Problems panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-has-been-checked-against\"\u003EWhat it has been checked against\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EClosed-form answers, with the sources in the test file tools/fem/test-solid.mjs: a cantilever against Timoshenko beam theory, self weight, a uniaxial patch test on a distorted mesh, Lame&#039;s thick-walled cylinder, mesh refinement, and the first bending frequency against Euler-Bernoulli. It was also run on the same mesh as PartCraft&#039;s own Simulation study and the two answers compared; the difference is written in the session log (docs/0.9-sessions/fem-09.md). It has not been compared with any other program, and no claim is made that it gives the same numbers as one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot a code check; your engineer reviews the results.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it solves Linear elastic, isotropic material, small displacements, static or natural frequencies. It does not do contact, yielding, large deformation, buckling, heat or flow. For yielding and contact use the Advanced simulation add-on. Reading the stress The stress is computed in each element and the stress tensors are averaged at the nodes they share; the plot and the first number use that average. The second number, unsmoothed, is the largest value found at an element node before averaging. When they differ a lot, the peak is sharp or the mesh is coarse there. Stress at a fixed face, at a point load and at a sharp inside corner grows as the mesh is refined, because the model has a singularity there. Refine the mesh (a smaller element size) and compare; do not read a single peak as the stress in the part. A pressure or force spread over a face is smoother than a fixed edge. The warnings Nothing holds the part, or it can still move or turn: it will not solve; add fixed faces. A coarse mesh (under 200 elements), stress above the yield stress, a displacement over 5 % of the part's size, or reactions that do not balance the loads: the result is shown with a warning that says what to do. The list also goes to the Problems panel. What it has been checked against Closed-form answers, with the sources in the test file tools/fem/test-solid.mjs: a cantilever against Timoshenko beam theory, self weight, a uniaxial patch test on a distorted mesh, Lame's thick-walled cylinder, mesh refinement, and the first bending frequency against Euler-Bernoulli. It was also run on the same mesh as PartCraft's own Simulation study and the two answers compared; the difference is written in the session log (docs/0.9-sessions/fem-09.md). It has not been compared with any other program, and no claim is made that it gives the same numbers as one. Not a code check; your engineer reviews the results."}]};
